From 08e01e0f71642f5dabe4d7ed60d35b2ccbac1edc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 18:31:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): follow an in-place Board gateway rename [daveey]

A Board gateway can now be renamed in place (`mini` -> `mac-mini-2`, same
key and hub.db; `hermes-mail rename-self` writes the mail config and hub.db
`meta.gateway`). Core assumed a gateway is never renamed. This makes core
correct across a rename; it renames nothing and writes neither file.

Why (five whys, short): parked probes and stored refs carry the gateway name
as text -> the name was treated as immutable -> a rename leaves them naming a
gateway that no longer has records -> wait-check reads an empty partition and
wait-comment misses the reply -> the parked card sleeps to its backstop.

What changed
- kanban_gateway_alias (new): the local alias resolver over
  `kanban.placement.aliases` (the map `canonical_pin` already reads). Config
  on disk only, no network. A name is replaced by its target only once the
  target has records in this host's hub.db, because the map is rolled out
  before the cutover.
- kanban_origin.gateway_name: the "never renamed in place" assumption is
  gone. The memo key already changes when the mail config is rewritten; the
  gap was a name read while config and `meta.gateway` disagree (rename half
  written), which was kept for 10 minutes. Such a read is no longer kept.
- kanban_origin.gateway_names: drops a renamed gateway's old partition (an
  alias of another known gateway) from the known set; never this gateway's
  own name. `resolve()` maps an origin ref written before the rename.
- wait-check `--gateway <former>`: the replica read uses the current name;
  the ssh destination stays the typed name (ssh aliases are their own
  namespace), with the current gateway's built-in route as fallback.
- wait-comment `--from-gateway <name>` (and the `hub:gw:<gateway>` author
  form, and the comment-will-not-wake warning): matches every name of that
  gateway, old and new.
- kanban_overlap host map: `mac-mini-2` is the machine `mini`.

Verified, not changed: review finding F10. A normal pull adopts a re-emitted
`tasks` row carrying a new `created_on` onto the existing local row, and
`own_partition` treats the card as own afterwards. The first full sync round
under the new name, run while every local row still says the old name,
sends 0 ops and deletes nothing (re-review R7 e), whichever gateway the
Board stamps as the re-emit's writer. Against the test FakeService, not the
real Board.

Proof
- RED at 4aa9d2a887 with only the new alias module present: 13 of 22 tests
  in test_kanban_gateway_rename.py fail; GREEN 22/22 after.
- test_kanban_sync_gateway_rename.py: 5/5, passing before and after (it
  verifies existing behaviour; no sync code changed).
- 150 test files that name the touched modules: 3,530 passed, 0 failed.
- Replay on this host's live hub.db (read-only, 7 partitions, live aliases
  `gateway-primary`, `gateway-small`): 0 names dropped from the known set, with and
  without the planned `mini: mac-mini-2` entry; `mini` still resolves to
  `mini` because `mac-mini-2` has no records yet.
- A plain rename (both files written) was already picked up by the old memo:
  that test passes before and after and is kept as the entry-path control.

Limits
- A `meta.gateway` edit that leaves the mail config untouched can still lag
  up to 10 minutes; nothing supported does that and hermes-hub rewrites
  `meta.gateway` from the config on every sync.
- `wait-check --gateway <own former name>` still goes over ssh to the typed
  host, as `--gateway <own name>` always has.
